<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="74b85e9f">&lt;p&gt;
    Consider the probability and impact of the identified risk using the guidance below. Calculate the risk priority for
    each risk by multiplying probability and impact levels. Compare the calculation to the risk factor matrix to determine
    risk severity. Note: Depending on the nature of the project, interpretations by level may need to be adjusted.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/Probability_table_600.jpg&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;135&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/Probability_table2_600.jpg&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;161&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.pm_med.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/Probability_table3_600.jpg&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;233&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Note: The criteria for setting the quality/scope, schedule/time and budget/cost impact factors will need to be adjusted
    to suit the client&apos;s requirements; they are not universal thresholds. The terms are coupled to reflect the
    interchangeability of the description. As an example, a person may refer to a quality aspect that encompasses the
    scope. Thus, if the scope of a project is the analysis of several operating systems, the time allowed for the
    assessment will impact the quality/scope of systems assessed (i.e., work that can be done in one week versus one
    month).&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Refer to below to calculate a risk factor value.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Risk factor priority calculation:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The risk factor priority is calculated as the product of the risk probability rating and impact rating when both are
    multiplied. For example, there is a risk that the vendor may not have the version of the software ready when the team
    is ready to install it. While the probability may be rated high (5) that the vendor cannot deliver on time, the impact
    could be low (1) if the old software is available and work is not delayed. Thus the risk factor priority factor is 5 [5
    (probablity rating) X 1 (impact rating)]. When compared to the risk factor priority matrix, &quot;5&quot; is plotted in the green
    area and represents low risk.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
